\paragraph{}
My ramblings on rocky ground got interrupted
when I stepped on something 
that was half buried in fine granular, 
then mounted upright and striked my shank. 
By taking hold of it and by its smell of the old grease, 
I've identified the femur of a vertebrate. 
So I started digging with my bare hands and 
hoeing the soil with my Louboutins
until an about 5ft long balloon-shaped capsule surfaced. The base material of the vessel was only partially exposed, 
most of it was wrapped in elastic black leatherette,
which maybe served as shock absorption 
and as extra sealant against contamination
that could bleed through the small orifice, which was giving access on the tapered side. 
The other side was embossed with inscriptions 
and I felt the phonetic signs, 
a sonorous manual to guide you safe and sound to the afterlife.
After scratching the ice from the dust-stained glass pane with my fringed fingernails, 
the unflattering picture of a shock frozen mummy
that I assumed in the fetal position appeared in my mind.
I dismissed a first strange thought, like if this posture would give away an
indication on how to navigate further and to avoid destiny. 


and no fix the hap-hazards of the depreciated simple complexity construct.

The glass casket was filled with wired electronical components, 
such as a Texas Instruments 555 timer chip, 
which controls the pulse of the artifice in obscure rhythm.


Such mobiles are not brought to you by the sun - 
the hidden strings apparently motivated by thin air -
are truly fascinating but unreachable for neglected little ones,
as they function completey different from spatio-temporal physical law.
In total it resembled an overpriced 'DIY' kit, 
which can be purchased by kids that are eager to please their slave masters, 
and just require blunt assemblage, 
but automated reaction to a simple given task represents 
accomplishment in the raging post-future wars of the zone.
Anyway, the femur got me to this sterile grave in the first instance.
Was it blood seepage from broken vessels
that our bones, like magnets, were on a collision course?

\paragraph{}
Was this mummified corpse too bloated for resurrection?
Had to get away away, but only to run in circles.
The once agonizing loss of direction faded, I let go.
taxidermy

I make out a speck of dust in a distance to find a
torn spacesuit about 30 yards away. Actually angry about myself, I blare 
"Where was your rational thinking then, private Unidentified, huh?"
"stripping down here in Cyberia?"
but on further consideration I mumbled remorsefully
"well, where there's foo, there's fire", and acknowledged
the late reactions to hyperthermia as not so stupid after all. 
So on coincide I find the crime scene both here and there, 
obviously the old puerility of apportion the blame. 
John Dee certainly was once a fit and experienced space explorer
but he lost his head here.

Two crystals replace the cosmonaut's once moist organs of sight, 
which ring at me from the skull. 

Eyes made out of atoms would fit my dark holes perfectly, I think to myself,
but wound is superior, it does not brag about status quo
because air
as it port towards the center of gravity 
eyes are excess garbage
light makes us do stoopid things

Now disgusted by the foul relic, 
I decide to go back as the dust yearns for mess, asking for it. 

At first I was concerned how to get it out, 
but then it was easier than thought, 
as the body had been previously dragged into the mud and therefore
not fully collapsed and more or less
indistinguishable from the environment.
On exhuming I'm 
piercing her ribcage, breaking violently into her
trunk, now only coated by a thin parchment
barrier, preventing the intrusion of dust.
How to connect my fibers to the new mind, the abdomen?
I decide to reconfigure the mummy in a new angle, 
which might be more dignified for conversation. 
But it turns out difficult not to break into pieces,
keeping the head upright, so the chin is parallel to the floor.
Thumb and index finger are pressed together,
to find stability in a X-legged position. 
I find a crack in her cranium, 
a deep incision, 
that reminded me of the medieval practice of opening a vent to drive away evil specter, would this hole have killed her in an instant and
was deadly on its own?


